Hi Krzysztof,

On Fri, 19 Aug 2005, Krzysztof Oledzki wrote:

> It seems that ipset -R does not handle properly "setname" in -A.
>
> This is a sample script that demonstrate my problem:
>
> --- cut here ---
> ipset -F;ipset -X;
>
> cat << EOF | ipset -R
>
> -N S1 macipmap --from 192.168.0.0 --to 192.168.31.255
> -N S2 macipmap --from 192.168.0.0 --to 192.168.31.255
>
> -A S1 192.168.0.17%00:01:E6:32:B6:82
> -A S2 192.168.0.15%00:11:43:5A:53:EC
> COMMIT
>
> EOF
>
> ipset -S
> --- cut here ---
>
>
> # ./ex
> # Generated by ipset 2.2.2 on Fri Aug 19 02:48:06 2005
> -N S1 macipmap --from 192.168.0.0 --to 192.168.31.255
> -N S2 macipmap --from 192.168.0.0 --to 192.168.31.255
> -A S2 192.168.0.15%00:11:43:5A:53:EC
> -A S2 192.168.0.17%00:01:E6:32:B6:82
> COMMIT
> # Completed on Fri Aug 19 02:48:06 2005
>
>
> Plase notice that my intention was to add 192.168.0.17%00:01:E6:32:B6:82
> into set S1, not into set S2.

That is partly a feature and partly a bug: ipset should have terminated
with an error message at the third line (the bug)

> -N S1 macipmap --from 192.168.0.0 --to 192.168.31.255
> -N S2 macipmap --from 192.168.0.0 --to 192.168.31.255
>
> -A S1 192.168.0.17%00:01:E6:32:B6:82

because the restore commands must follow each other in a rigid order (the
feature): define the set, add all elements, *then* define next set and add
its elements. So the right order of the commands is

> -N S1 macipmap --from 192.168.0.0 --to 192.168.31.255
> -A S1 192.168.0.17%00:01:E6:32:B6:82
> -N S2 macipmap --from 192.168.0.0 --to 192.168.31.255
> -A S2 192.168.0.15%00:11:43:5A:53:EC

The corrected check to ensure the proper ordering will be added to the
next release.

